IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Interpretation Act 1984 (Western Australia), Act No. 12 of 1984 -- LAYER 1: TIME.
--
-- The time rules of the Act in one self-contained module (it imports only `prelude` and `daydate`):
--   * when a written law comes into operation, and at what moment of the day
--     (ss 2, 3(3), 20, 21, 22, 23, 25(3), 27, 41);
--   * how a period of days, months or years is counted, which day is its last, and what happens when that
--     day is an excluded day (ss 5 "month" and "year", 61, 62, 63, 64);
--   * the times the Act itself gives words (s 5 "financial year" and "year"; "day" and "week" are NOT defined);
--   * judicial acts and service of process on any day (s 48A);
--   * when service by post is taken to be effected (s 75(1)).
-- Every rule is written from the text of the Act alone.  Where the text does not answer, the result is NOTHING
-- (the explicit "not covered" value) and a test asserts it.  Borrowed facts (which dates are public holidays,
-- bank holidays or public service holidays; the day a letter would have been delivered in the ordinary course
-- of post) are plain inputs.
--
-- The text read here is a plain-text rendering made by a converter script; it is not the official text.
--
-- Names are prefixed by what they are about so that no other module of this encoding defines the same name.

§ `Interpretation Act 1984 (WA) -- time`

-- Dates are written `Date day month year`; this encoding defines no YMD or YearMonthDay shim.

§§ `The types this module uses`

-- The unit a period is counted in.  The Act defines "month" (s 62) and "year" (s 5); it defines neither "day"
-- nor "week".
DECLARE `Unit of a period` IS ONE OF
    `in days`
    `in weeks`
    `in months`
    `in years`

DECLARE `Period of time` HAS
    `number of units` IS A NUMBER
    `unit`            IS A `Unit of a period`

-- Section 61(1)(a) and (b): how the text ties a period to a named day.
DECLARE `How a period is tied to a day` IS ONE OF
    `begins at, on or with the day`
    `is reckoned from or after the day`

-- Section 61(2): the days that are "excluded days" besides Saturday and Sunday.
-- Which dates are public service holidays, bank holidays or public holidays is a borrowed fact
-- (s 5 refers to the Public Service Act 1978 and to the Public and Bank Holidays Act 1972, which are not among
-- the sources): the person states the dates.  A public half-holiday is a PART of a day (s 5) and is not listed in
-- s 61(2), so it is not an excluded day.
DECLARE `Holidays that make an excluded day` HAS
    `public service holidays`            IS A LIST OF DATE
    `bank holidays in the relevant area`   IS A LIST OF DATE
    `public holidays in the relevant area` IS A LIST OF DATE

DECLARE `End of a period` HAS
    `last day by the calendar`     IS A DATE
    `last day for doing the thing` IS A DATE

DECLARE `Reckoned period` HAS
    `first day` IS A DATE
    `end`       IS A `End of a period`

-- Section 61(1)(f) and (g).
DECLARE `How a count of days between two events is expressed` IS ONE OF
    `clear days`
    `at least or not less than a number of days`
    `a number of days not expressed in either of those ways`

DECLARE `Word for a length of time` IS ONE OF
    `the word day`
    `the word week`
    `the word month`
    `the word year`
    `the words financial year`

DECLARE `Meaning the Act gives a time word` IS ONE OF
    `a calendar month, reckoned under section 62`
    `a period of 12 months`
    `the period of 12 months ending on 30 June`

-- Section 20.
DECLARE `How an Act received assent` IS ONE OF
    `Royal Assent given`
    `reserved for the signification of Her Majesty's pleasure`

DECLARE `What an Act itself says about when it commences` IS ONE OF
    `nothing about it`
    `a particular day` HAS `the particular day` IS A DATE
    `a day to be fixed by proclamation` HAS `the day fixed, if fixed yet` IS A MAYBE DATE

-- Section 21.
DECLARE `Moment of a day` HAS
    `the calendar day`  IS A DATE
    `second of the day` IS A NUMBER

-- Section 27.
DECLARE `Commencement of one provision` HAS
    `the provision`        IS A STRING
    `its commencement day` IS A DATE

§§ `Section 2 and section 3(3): the Act's own day, and which laws the time-gated sections reach`

-- src:114 | This Act shall come into operation on 1 July 1984.
@ref s 2 (src:114)
GIVETH A DATE
`the day this Act came into operation` MEANS Date 1 7 1984

-- Section 3(3) removes from "passed or made after the commencement of this Act", in ss 17, 25, 43(6), 50 and
-- 64, any enactment that continues or directly amends, but does not repeal entirely, the text of an existing
-- written law.  The Act comes into operation at the beginning of its commencement day (s 21), so a law passed
-- or made ON that day is passed or made after the commencement (fork F4 in NOTES.md).
-- src:121 | (3) A reference in section 17, 25, 43(6), 50 or 64 to an Act, written law, enactment, or subsidiary legislation passed or made after the commencement of this Act shall be construed so as not to include any enactment which continues or directly amends, but does not repeal entirely, the text of an existing written law 1.
@ref s 3(3) (src:121)
GIVEN `the day the law was passed or made` IS A DATE
      `it continues or directly amends, but does not repeal entirely, the text of an existing written law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`counts as passed or made after the commencement for ss 17, 25, 43(6), 50 and 64` MEANS
        Day `the day the law was passed or made` AT LEAST Day `the day this Act came into operation`
    AND (NOT `it continues or directly amends, but does not repeal entirely, the text of an existing written law`)

§§ `Section 5: what the Act says the words for a length of time mean`

-- src:198 | month has the meaning given in section 62;
-- src:256 | year means a period of 12 months.
-- src:171 | financial year means the period of 12 months ending on 30 June;
@ref s 5 "month" (src:198)
@ref s 5 "year" (src:256)
@ref s 5 "financial year" (src:171)
GIVEN w IS A `Word for a length of time`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Meaning the Act gives a time word`
`what the Act says a word for a length of time means` w MEANS
    CONSIDER w
    WHEN `the word day` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `the word week` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `the word month` THEN JUST `a calendar month, reckoned under section 62`
    WHEN `the word year` THEN JUST `a period of 12 months`
    WHEN `the words financial year` THEN JUST `the period of 12 months ending on 30 June`

-- src:171 | financial year means the period of 12 months ending on 30 June;
@ref s 5 "financial year" (src:171)
GIVEN `the calendar year in which the financial year ends` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day of the financial year ending in` `the calendar year in which the financial year ends` MEANS
    Date 1 7 (`the calendar year in which the financial year ends` MINUS 1)

-- src:171 | financial year means the period of 12 months ending on 30 June;
@ref s 5 "financial year" (src:171)
GIVEN `the calendar year in which the financial year ends` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of the financial year ending in` `the calendar year in which the financial year ends` MEANS
    Date 30 6 `the calendar year in which the financial year ends`

-- src:171 | financial year means the period of 12 months ending on 30 June;
@ref s 5 "financial year" (src:171)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the calendar year in which the financial year containing the date ends` d MEANS
    IF DATE_MONTH d AT LEAST 7 THEN DATE_YEAR d PLUS 1 ELSE DATE_YEAR d

§§ `Part III -- when a written law comes into operation`

-- Section 20.  The "28th day after the day on which that Act receives the Royal Assent": the day of assent is
-- excluded (s 61(1)(b), "after"), so the 28th day after is the day of assent plus 28.
-- src:371 | (1) Every Act to which the Royal Assent has been given before 1 July 1984 shall be deemed to have come into operation on the day on which that Act received the Royal Assent, unless the contrary intention appears in the Act.
-- src:372 | (2) Every Act to which the Royal Assent is given on or after 1 July 1984 shall, unless the contrary intention appears in that Act, come into operation on the 28th day after the day on which that Act receives the Royal Assent.
-- src:373 | (3) Every Act reserved for the signification of Her Majesty’s pleasure on or after 1 July 1984 shall, unless the contrary intention appears in that Act, come into operation on the day on which Her Majesty’s assent is proclaimed in the Gazette.
@ref s 20 (src:371-373)
GIVEN how IS A `How an Act received assent`
      `the day of Royal Assent, or the day the Act was reserved` IS A DATE
      `the day Her Majesty's assent was proclaimed in the Gazette, if it has been` IS A MAYBE DATE
      itself IS A `What an Act itself says about when it commences`
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the day an Act comes into operation` how `the day of Royal Assent, or the day the Act was reserved` `the day Her Majesty's assent was proclaimed in the Gazette, if it has been` itself MEANS
    CONSIDER itself
    WHEN `a particular day` fixed THEN JUST fixed
    WHEN `a day to be fixed by proclamation` proclaimed THEN proclaimed
    OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER how
        WHEN `Royal Assent given` THEN
            IF Day `the day of Royal Assent, or the day the Act was reserved` LESS THAN Day `the day this Act came into operation`
            THEN JUST `the day of Royal Assent, or the day the Act was reserved`
            ELSE JUST (`the day of Royal Assent, or the day the Act was reserved` PLUS 28)
        WHEN `reserved for the signification of Her Majesty's pleasure` THEN
            IF Day `the day of Royal Assent, or the day the Act was reserved` LESS THAN Day `the day this Act came into operation`
            THEN NOTHING
            ELSE `the day Her Majesty's assent was proclaimed in the Gazette, if it has been`

-- Section 21: a written law that comes into operation on a particular day does so at the beginning of that day.
-- src:375 | Where any written law, or portion of a written law, comes into operation on a particular day, it shall come into operation at the beginning of that day.
@ref s 21 (src:375)
GIVEN `the day it comes into operation` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Moment of a day`
`the moment a written law comes into operation` `the day it comes into operation` MEANS
    `Moment of a day` WITH
        `the calendar day`  IS `the day it comes into operation`
        `second of the day` IS 0

-- src:375 | Where any written law, or portion of a written law, comes into operation on a particular day, it shall come into operation at the beginning of that day.
@ref s 21 (src:375)
GIVEN `the moment asked about` IS A `Moment of a day`
      `the day it comes into operation` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a written law is in operation at` `the moment asked about` `given that it comes into operation on` `the day it comes into operation` MEANS
    ((Day (`the moment asked about`'s `the calendar day`)) TIMES 86400 PLUS `the moment asked about`'s `second of the day`)
        AT LEAST ((Day `the day it comes into operation`) TIMES 86400)

-- Section 22: the provision that makes the Act come into operation on a day to be fixed by proclamation, and the
-- short title, come into operation on the day of Royal Assent unless expressly provided otherwise.
-- src:377 | Notwithstanding section 20(2), where an Act provides that the Act, or portion of the Act, is to come into operation on a day to be fixed by proclamation, that provision and the provision providing for the short title of the Act, unless it is otherwise expressly provided, shall come into operation on the day on which the Act receives the Royal Assent.
@ref s 22 (src:377)
GIVEN `the day of Royal Assent` IS A DATE
      `the day expressly provided for them, if any` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day the proclamation provision and the short title come into operation` `the day of Royal Assent` `the day expressly provided for them, if any` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the day expressly provided for them, if any`
    WHEN JUST other THEN other
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `the day of Royal Assent`

-- Section 23(a): a power to fix a day for an Act to come into operation does not include power to fix a day
-- prior to the day on which the proclamation is published in the Gazette or on the WA legislation website,
-- unless express provision is made.  The publication day itself is not "prior" to it.
-- src:379 | A power to fix a day on which an Act shall come into operation does not include power to fix —
-- src:380 | (a) a day prior to the day on which the proclamation fixing the day is published in the Gazette or on the WA legislation website; or
-- src:381 | (b) different days for different provisions of that Act,
-- src:382 | unless express provision is made in that behalf.
@ref s 23 (src:379-382)
@ref s 23(a) (src:380)
GIVEN `the day the proclamation is published` IS A DATE
      `the day it fixes` IS A DATE
      `express provision is made that it may fix an earlier day` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the power to fix a day extends to a day not prior to publication` `the day the proclamation is published` `the day it fixes` `express provision is made that it may fix an earlier day` MEANS
        Day `the day it fixes` AT LEAST Day `the day the proclamation is published`
    OR  `express provision is made that it may fix an earlier day`

-- Section 23(b): nor does it include power to fix different days for different provisions, unless express
-- provision is made.
@ref s 23(b) (src:381)
GIVEN `the number of different days fixed` IS A NUMBER
      `express provision is made for different days` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the power to fix a day extends to the number of different days fixed` `the number of different days fixed` `express provision is made for different days` MEANS
        `the number of different days fixed` AT MOST 1
    OR  `express provision is made for different days`

-- Section 25(3): an instrument made, or a notice or document given or served, under a power exercised before the
-- provision conferring it commences takes effect on the later of (a) the day the provision commences and (b) the
-- day it would have taken effect had the provision already commenced when the instrument was made or the notice
-- or document was given or served.
-- src:402 | (3) Where a power to make an instrument of a legislative or administrative character, or to give or serve a notice or other document, is exercised as provided in subsection (1) or (2), that instrument, notice, or document shall take effect —
-- src:403 | (a) on the day on which the provision referred to in subsection (1) or, as the case may be, the provision firstmentioned in subsection (2) commences; or
-- src:404 | (b) on the day on which it would have taken effect, if at the time when the instrument was made or the notice or document was given or served, the provision so mentioned or firstmentioned had commenced,
-- src:405 | whichever is the later.
@ref s 25(3) (src:402-405)
GIVEN `the day the provision commences` IS A DATE
      `the day it would have taken effect had the provision already commenced` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day an instrument or notice made early takes effect` `the day the provision commences` `the day it would have taken effect had the provision already commenced` MEANS
    IF Day `the day the provision commences` AT LEAST Day `the day it would have taken effect had the provision already commenced`
    THEN `the day the provision commences`
    ELSE `the day it would have taken effect had the provision already commenced`

-- Section 27: where different provisions of a written law come into operation on different days, a reference to
-- its commencement is to the day of coming into operation of the appropriate provisions.  The Act does not say
-- which provisions are appropriate: the caller names them, and a provision that is not listed is not covered.
-- src:417 | A reference in a written law to the day of coming into operation or to the commencement of a written law shall, where different provisions of the written law come or came into operation on different days, be construed as a reference to the day of coming into operation of the appropriate provisions of that written law.
@ref s 27 (src:417)
GIVEN `the provision the reference is about` IS A STRING
      `the commencement days of the provisions` IS A LIST OF `Commencement of one provision`
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the day a reference to commencement means` `the provision the reference is about` `the commencement days of the provisions` MEANS
    CONSIDER filter (GIVEN c YIELD c's `the provision` EQUALS `the provision the reference is about`) `the commencement days of the provisions`
    WHEN c FOLLOWED BY rest THEN JUST (c's `its commencement day`)
    WHEN EMPTY THEN NOTHING

-- Section 41(1)(b): subsidiary legislation comes into operation, subject to s 42, on the day of publication or,
-- where another day is specified or provided for in it, on that day.  Unlike s 23(a) for an Act, the text bars no day before
-- publication: the specified day is returned whatever it is (fork F13 in NOTES.md).
-- src:470 | (1) Where a written law confers power to make subsidiary legislation, all subsidiary legislation made under that power shall —
-- src:471 | (a) be published in the Gazette or on the WA legislation website; and
-- src:472 | (b) subject to section 42, come into operation on the day of publication, or where another day is specified or provided for in the subsidiary legislation, on that day.
@ref s 41(1) (src:470)
@ref s 41(1)(b) (src:472)
GIVEN `the day it is published in the Gazette or on the WA legislation website` IS A DATE
      `another day specified or provided for in it, if any` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day subsidiary legislation comes into operation` `the day it is published in the Gazette or on the WA legislation website` `another day specified or provided for in it, if any` MEANS
    CONSIDER `another day specified or provided for in it, if any`
    WHEN JUST other THEN other
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `the day it is published in the Gazette or on the WA legislation website`

-- Section 41(2): a power to fix a day for subsidiary legislation to come into operation does not include power
-- to fix different days for different provisions unless express provision is made.
-- src:473 | (2) A power to fix a day on which subsidiary legislation shall come into operation does not include power to fix different days for different provisions of that legislation unless express provision is made in that behalf.
@ref s 41(2) (src:473)
GIVEN `the number of different days fixed` IS A NUMBER
      `express provision is made for different days` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the power to fix a day for subsidiary legislation extends to the number of different days fixed` `the number of different days fixed` `express provision is made for different days` MEANS
        `the number of different days fixed` AT MOST 1
    OR  `express provision is made for different days`

§§ `Part VII -- section 48A: judicial acts and service of process on any day`

-- Section 48A: a power to act judicially (including taking a verdict), or to serve a document or court process
-- or execute a warrant, may be exercised on any day of the year.  A day that is an excluded day under s 61(2)
-- is not a bar.  (The Act does not say what makes a document "court process"; that is the caller's fact.)
-- src:559 | Where a written or other law confers on a person power to —
-- src:560 | (a) act judicially (including taking a verdict); or
-- src:561 | (b) serve any document or court process or execute any warrant,
-- src:562 | that power may be exercised on any day of the year.
@ref s 48A (src:559-562)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      `the power is to act judicially, serve a document or court process, or execute a warrant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the power may be exercised on` d `if` `the power is to act judicially, serve a document or court process, or execute a warrant` MEANS
    IF `the power is to act judicially, serve a document or court process, or execute a warrant`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Part VIII -- sections 61 and 62: time, computation of; months, reckoning of`

-- Section 61(2): excluded day means Saturday, Sunday, public service holiday, and a bank holiday or public
-- holiday throughout the State or in that part of the State which is relevant to the event, act, thing or
-- proceeding concerned.
-- src:641 | (2) For the purposes of this section, excluded day means Saturday, Sunday, public service holiday, and a bank holiday or public holiday throughout the State or in that part of the State which is relevant to the event, act, thing or proceeding concerned.
@ref s 61(2) (src:641)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      h IS A `Holidays that make an excluded day`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is an excluded day` d h MEANS
        `is weekend` d
    OR  elem d (h's `public service holidays`)
    OR  elem d (h's `bank holidays in the relevant area`)
    OR  elem d (h's `public holidays in the relevant area`)

-- Section 61(1)(e) and (h): where the time limited for the doing of a thing expires or falls upon an excluded day,
-- the thing may be done on the next day that is not an excluded day.
-- src:637 | (e) where the time limited for the doing of a thing expires or falls upon an excluded day, the thing may be done on the next day that is not an excluded day;
-- src:640 | (h) where an act or proceeding is directed or allowed to be done or taken on a certain day, or on or before a certain day, then, if that day is an excluded day, the act or proceeding shall be considered as done or taken in due time if it is done or taken on the next day that is not an excluded day.
@ref s 61(1)(e) (src:637)
@ref s 61(1)(h) (src:640)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      h IS A `Holidays that make an excluded day`
GIVETH A DATE
`the next day that is not an excluded day, counting the day itself` d h MEANS
    IF `is an excluded day` d h
    THEN `the next day that is not an excluded day, counting the day itself` (d PLUS 1) h
    ELSE d

-- Section 61(1)(a) and (b): the first day of a period.  A period expressed to begin at, on or with a specified
-- day includes that day; one expressed to be reckoned from, or after, a specified day does not.
-- src:633 | (a) where a period of time is expressed to begin at, on, or with a specified day, that day shall be included in the period;
-- src:634 | (b) where a period of time is expressed to be reckoned from, or after, a specified day, that day shall not be included in the period;
@ref s 61(1)(a) (src:633)
@ref s 61(1)(b) (src:634)
GIVEN tie IS A `How a period is tied to a day`
      named IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day of a period` tie named MEANS
    CONSIDER tie
    WHEN `begins at, on or with the day` THEN named
    WHEN `is reckoned from or after the day` THEN named PLUS 1

-- Section 62(2) and (3): a period of one month, or of 2, 3 or more months, beginning on a date is reckoned from
-- that date to the date numerically corresponding, less one, in the next (or the second, third, or other
-- successive) month, or, if there is no corresponding date, to the last day of that month.
-- `add months` CLAMPS to the last day of a short month; the clamp is detected by the day of the month changing,
-- and in that case there is no corresponding date and the clamped day IS the answer (no "less one").
-- A period beginning on the first day of a month needs no special case: the corresponding date is the first of
-- the later month, and "less one" is the last day of the month before it, which is the calendar month of s 62(1).
-- src:643 | (1) In a written law, month means a calendar month, that is to say, a month reckoned according to the calendar.
-- src:644 | (2) If a period of one month indicated in a written law begins on any date other than the first day of any of the 12 months of the calendar, it shall be reckoned from the date on which it is to begin to the date in the next month numerically corresponding, less one, or, if there is no corresponding date, to the last day of that month.
-- src:645 | For example: a month beginning on 15 January ends on 14 February and a month beginning on 30 or 31 January ends on 28 February (or 29 February in a leap year).
-- src:646 | (3) If a period indicated in a written law is of 2, 3 or more months, it shall be reckoned from the date on which it is to begin to the date numerically corresponding, less one, in the second, third, or other successive month thereafter or, if there is no such corresponding date, to the last day of the latter month.
@ref s 62(1) (src:643)
@ref s 62(2) (src:644)
@ref s 62(3) (src:646)
GIVEN `the first day` IS A DATE
      `the number of months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of a period of months beginning on` `the first day` `the number of months` MEANS
    IF DATE_DAY target EQUALS DATE_DAY `the first day`
    THEN Date (Day target MINUS 1)
    ELSE target
    WHERE
        target MEANS `add months` `the first day` `the number of months`

-- Section 61(1)(e), (h) with s 62: the end of a period whose last day is as stated.
-- src:637 | (e) where the time limited for the doing of a thing expires or falls upon an excluded day, the thing may be done on the next day that is not an excluded day;
@ref s 61(1)(e) (src:637)
GIVEN `the last day by the calendar` IS A DATE
      h IS A `Holidays that make an excluded day`
GIVETH A `End of a period`
`the end of a period whose last day by the calendar is` `the last day by the calendar` h MEANS
    `End of a period` WITH
        `last day by the calendar`     IS `the last day by the calendar`
        `last day for doing the thing` IS `the next day that is not an excluded day, counting the day itself` `the last day by the calendar` h

-- Section 61 and section 62 together: the period asked about, tied to a named day as the text says, counted in
-- days (the first day counts as day 1), months (s 62) or years ("year means a period of 12 months", s 5, so N
-- years are 12N months under s 62(3)).  The Act says nothing about how to count weeks: NOTHING.  A count that is
-- not a whole number of at least 1 is not a period the Act counts: NOTHING.
-- src:632 | (1) In computing time for the purposes of a written law —
-- src:633 | (a) where a period of time is expressed to begin at, on, or with a specified day, that day shall be included in the period;
-- src:634 | (b) where a period of time is expressed to be reckoned from, or after, a specified day, that day shall not be included in the period;
-- src:641 | (2) For the purposes of this section, excluded day means Saturday, Sunday, public service holiday, and a bank holiday or public holiday throughout the State or in that part of the State which is relevant to the event, act, thing or proceeding concerned.
-- src:643 | (1) In a written law, month means a calendar month, that is to say, a month reckoned according to the calendar.
-- src:644 | (2) If a period of one month indicated in a written law begins on any date other than the first day of any of the 12 months of the calendar, it shall be reckoned from the date on which it is to begin to the date in the next month numerically corresponding, less one, or, if there is no corresponding date, to the last day of that month.
-- src:645 | For example: a month beginning on 15 January ends on 14 February and a month beginning on 30 or 31 January ends on 28 February (or 29 February in a leap year).
-- src:646 | (3) If a period indicated in a written law is of 2, 3 or more months, it shall be reckoned from the date on which it is to begin to the date numerically corresponding, less one, in the second, third, or other successive month thereafter or, if there is no such corresponding date, to the last day of the latter month.
-- src:256 | year means a period of 12 months.
@ref s 61(1)(a) (src:633)
@ref s 61(1)(b) (src:634)
@ref s 62 (src:643-646)
@ref s 5 "year" (src:256)
GIVEN p IS A `Period of time`
      tie IS A `How a period is tied to a day`
      named IS A DATE
      h IS A `Holidays that make an excluded day`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Reckoned period`
`reckon a period` p tie named h MEANS
    IF p's `number of units` AT LEAST 1 AND FLOOR (p's `number of units`) EQUALS p's `number of units`
    THEN
        CONSIDER p's `unit`
        WHEN `in days` THEN
            JUST (`Reckoned period` WITH
                      `first day` IS first
                      `end`       IS `the end of a period whose last day by the calendar is` (first PLUS (p's `number of units` MINUS 1)) h)
        WHEN `in weeks` THEN NOTHING
        WHEN `in months` THEN
            JUST (`Reckoned period` WITH
                      `first day` IS first
                      `end`       IS `the end of a period whose last day by the calendar is` (`the last day of a period of months beginning on` first (p's `number of units`)) h)
        WHEN `in years` THEN
            JUST (`Reckoned period` WITH
                      `first day` IS first
                      `end`       IS `the end of a period whose last day by the calendar is` (`the last day of a period of months beginning on` first (p's `number of units` TIMES 12)) h)
    ELSE NOTHING
    WHERE
        first MEANS `the first day of a period` tie named

-- The OTHER reading of the fork in s 61(1)(b) / s 62 (F1 in NOTES.md), shown so that the answer table can print
-- both columns: that s 62 runs from the named day itself ("the date on which it is to begin").  NOT the reading
-- taken by `reckon a period`.
@ref s 62 (src:644)
GIVEN p IS A `Period of time`
      named IS A DATE
      h IS A `Holidays that make an excluded day`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Reckoned period`
`reckon a period counting the named day as its first day, the other reading` p named h MEANS
    `reckon a period` p `begins at, on or with the day` named h

-- Section 61(1)(c): where anything is to be done within a time before a specified day, the time does not include
-- that day.  Only a time in days can be counted back from a day: the Act's s 62 reckons forward from a date, so a
-- time in months or years before a day is not covered.
-- src:635 | (c) where anything is to be done within a time before a specified day, the time shall not include that day;
@ref s 61(1)(c) (src:635)
GIVEN p IS A `Period of time`
      named IS A DATE
      h IS A `Holidays that make an excluded day`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Reckoned period`
`the time within a time before a day` p named h MEANS
    IF p's `number of units` AT LEAST 1 AND FLOOR (p's `number of units`) EQUALS p's `number of units`
    THEN
        CONSIDER p's `unit`
        WHEN `in days` THEN
            JUST (`Reckoned period` WITH
                      `first day` IS named MINUS p's `number of units`
                      `end`       IS `the end of a period whose last day by the calendar is` (named MINUS 1) h)
        OTHERWISE NOTHING
    ELSE NOTHING

-- The OTHER reading of the fork in s 61(1)(c) with s 61(1)(e) (F3 in NOTES.md): that (c) wins, so a time before a named day
-- whose last day by the calendar is an excluded day must be used up on the last day that is not an excluded day BEFORE it,
-- and (e) is not allowed to carry the thing forward to or past the named day.  NOT the reading taken by
-- `the time within a time before a day`, which applies (e) as written: 10 days before Sunday 11 February 2024 end on Saturday
-- 10 February by the calendar, and the thing may then be done on Monday 12 February, the day AFTER the named day.
-- This function ends the same period on Friday 9 February.
-- src:635 | (c) where anything is to be done within a time before a specified day, the time shall not include that day;
-- src:637 | (e) where the time limited for the doing of a thing expires or falls upon an excluded day, the thing may be done on the next day that is not an excluded day;
@ref s 61(1)(c) (src:635)
@ref s 61(1)(e) (src:637)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      h IS A `Holidays that make an excluded day`
GIVETH A DATE
`the previous day that is not an excluded day, counting the day itself` d h MEANS
    IF `is an excluded day` d h
    THEN `the previous day that is not an excluded day, counting the day itself` (d MINUS 1) h
    ELSE d

@ref s 61(1)(c) (src:635)
GIVEN p IS A `Period of time`
      named IS A DATE
      h IS A `Holidays that make an excluded day`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Reckoned period`
`the time within a time before a day, rolling back from an excluded last day, the other reading` p named h MEANS
    IF p's `number of units` AT LEAST 1 AND FLOOR (p's `number of units`) EQUALS p's `number of units`
    THEN
        CONSIDER p's `unit`
        WHEN `in days` THEN
            JUST (`Reckoned period` WITH
                      `first day` IS named MINUS p's `number of units`
                      `end`       IS `End of a period` WITH
                                         `last day by the calendar`     IS named MINUS 1
                                         `last day for doing the thing` IS `the previous day that is not an excluded day, counting the day itself` (named MINUS 1) h)
        OTHERWISE NOTHING
    ELSE NOTHING

-- Section 61(1)(d): a period expressed to end at, on or with a specified day, or to continue to or until it,
-- includes that day: it is the last day of the period.
-- src:636 | (d) where a period of time is expressed to end at, on, or with a specified day or to continue to or until a specified day, that day shall be included in the period;
@ref s 61(1)(d) (src:636)
GIVEN named IS A DATE
      h IS A `Holidays that make an excluded day`
GIVETH A `End of a period`
`the end of a period expressed to end on` named h MEANS
    `the end of a period whose last day by the calendar is` named h

-- Section 61(1)(f) and (g): a number of days between two events.
-- src:638 | (f) where there is a reference to a number of clear days or “at least” or “not less than” a number of days between 2 events, in calculating that number of days both the days on which the events happen shall be excluded;
-- src:639 | (g) where there is a reference to a number of days not expressed to be clear days or “at least” or “not less than” a number of days between 2 events, in calculating the number of days there shall be excluded the day on which the first event happens and there shall be included the day on which the second event happens;
@ref s 61(1)(f) (src:638)
@ref s 61(1)(g) (src:639)
GIVEN how IS A `How a count of days between two events is expressed`
      `the day the first event happens` IS A DATE
      `the day the second event happens` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of days counted between two events` how `the day the first event happens` `the day the second event happens` MEANS
    CONSIDER how
    WHEN `clear days` THEN (Day `the day the second event happens` MINUS Day `the day the first event happens`) MINUS 1
    WHEN `at least or not less than a number of days` THEN (Day `the day the second event happens` MINUS Day `the day the first event happens`) MINUS 1
    WHEN `a number of days not expressed in either of those ways` THEN Day `the day the second event happens` MINUS Day `the day the first event happens`

§§ `Part VIII -- section 63: no time fixed; section 64: extension after the time has expired`

-- Section 63: where no time is fixed or allowed within which an act or thing shall be done, it shall be done
-- with all convenient speed and as often as occasion arises.  "All convenient speed" is open-textured; the Act
-- gives no day, so there is no last day to compute and the result is NOTHING.
-- src:649 | Where no time is fixed or allowed within which an act or thing shall be done, such act or thing shall be done with all convenient speed and as often as occasion arises.
@ref s 63 (src:649)
GIVEN `a time is fixed or allowed for doing the act or thing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the last day if one is fixed` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the last day for an act or thing when no time may be fixed` `a time is fixed or allowed for doing the act or thing` `the last day if one is fixed` MEANS
    IF `a time is fixed or allowed for doing the act or thing` THEN JUST `the last day if one is fixed` ELSE NOTHING

-- src:649 | Where no time is fixed or allowed within which an act or thing shall be done, such act or thing shall be done with all convenient speed and as often as occasion arises.
@ref s 63 (src:649)
GIVEN `a time is fixed or allowed for doing the act or thing` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the act or thing must be done with all convenient speed and as often as occasion arises` `a time is fixed or allowed for doing the act or thing` MEANS
    NOT (`a time is fixed or allowed for doing the act or thing`)

-- Section 64(1): where a time is fixed for doing an act or taking a proceeding and power is given to a court or
-- other authority to extend it, the power may be exercised although the application for extension is not made
-- until after the time has expired.  Section 64(2) with s 3(3): the section applies to written laws passed or
-- made after the commencement of this Act; for any other law the Act is silent (NOTHING).
-- src:651 | (1) Where in a written law a time is fixed or allowed for doing any act or thing or taking any proceeding and power is given to a court or other authority to extend that time, such power may be exercised by the court or other authority although the application for an extension is not made until after the expiration of the time fixed or allowed.
-- src:652 | (2) Subject to section 3(3), this section applies to written laws passed or made after the commencement of this Act.
@ref s 64 (src:651-652)
GIVEN `the power to extend the time was given to a court or other authority` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day the law giving the power was passed or made` IS A DATE
      `it continues or directly amends, but does not repeal entirely, the text of an existing written law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`an application to extend may be made after the time has expired` `the power to extend the time was given to a court or other authority` `the day the law giving the power was passed or made` `it continues or directly amends, but does not repeal entirely, the text of an existing written law` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the power to extend the time was given to a court or other authority`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE
        IF `counts as passed or made after the commencement for ss 17, 25, 43(6), 50 and 64` `the day the law giving the power was passed or made` `it continues or directly amends, but does not repeal entirely, the text of an existing written law`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Part X -- section 75(1): service by post`

-- Section 75(1): where a written law authorises or requires a document to be served by post, service is deemed to
-- be effected by properly addressing and posting (by prepaid post) the document as a letter to the last known
-- address of the person to be served, and, unless the contrary is proved, to have been effected at the time when
-- the letter would have been delivered in the ordinary course of post.  The Act does not say how long the ordinary
-- course of post takes: the caller states the day.  If the contrary is proved, the Act does not give a rule; the
-- day proved is taken (fork F7 in NOTES.md).
-- src:704 | (1) Where a written law authorises or requires a document to be served by post, whether the word “serve” or any of the words “give”, “deliver”, or “send” or any other similar word or expression is used, service shall be deemed to be effected by properly addressing and posting (by prepaid post) the document as a letter to the last known address of the person to be served, and, unless the contrary is proved, to have been effected at the time when the letter would have been delivered in the ordinary course of post.
@ref s 75(1) (src:704)
GIVEN `it was properly addressed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was posted by prepaid post` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was posted as a letter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was addressed to the last known address of the person to be served` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day it would have been delivered in the ordinary course of post` IS A DATE
      `the day of delivery, if the contrary is proved` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the day service by post is deemed effected` `it was properly addressed` `it was posted by prepaid post` `it was posted as a letter` `it was addressed to the last known address of the person to be served` `the day it would have been delivered in the ordinary course of post` `the day of delivery, if the contrary is proved` MEANS
    IF     `it was properly addressed`
       AND `it was posted by prepaid post`
       AND `it was posted as a letter`
       AND `it was addressed to the last known address of the person to be served`
    THEN
        CONSIDER `the day of delivery, if the contrary is proved`
        WHEN JUST proved THEN JUST proved
        WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST `the day it would have been delivered in the ordinary course of post`
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Part X -- section 77(4): the Second Schedule to the Interpretation Act 1918, sections B to E, time to complain`

-- Section 77(4) keeps the Second Schedule to the 1918 Act, with s 47(2) of that Act, in force for Acts passed before 1 July
-- 1984 to which s 47 applied.  Sections B to E of that Schedule fix how long after an offence the complaint must be made
-- and the proceedings commenced: one, three, six or twelve months.  A time is reckoned after the day the offence was
-- committed (s 61(1)(b), the first day is the next day), by calendar months (s 62), and an end that falls on an excluded
-- day rolls to the next day that is not (s 61(1)(e)).  Twelve months is the same as a year (s 5).
-- src:951 | B.
-- src:952 | The complaint in respect of an offence against this Act shall be made and the proceedings commenced within one month after the offence thereby charged shall have been committed.
-- src:953 | C.
-- src:954 | The complaint in respect of an offence against this Act shall be made and the proceedings commenced within three months after the offence thereby charged shall have been committed.
-- src:955 | D.
-- src:956 | The complaint in respect of an offence against this Act shall be made and the proceedings commenced within six months after the offence thereby charged shall have been committed.
-- src:957 | E.
-- src:958 | The complaint in respect of an offence against this Act shall be made and the proceedings commenced within twelve months after the offence thereby charged shall have been committed.
@ref note 4 Second Schedule B to E (src:951-958)
DECLARE `Section of the Second Schedule that fixes a time to complain` IS ONE OF
    `section B -- one month`
    `section C -- three months`
    `section D -- six months`
    `section E -- twelve months`

GIVEN s IS A `Section of the Second Schedule that fixes a time to complain`
GIVETH A `Period of time`
`the period the Second Schedule section allows` s MEANS
    CONSIDER s
    WHEN `section B -- one month` THEN
        `Period of time` WITH
            `number of units` IS 1
            `unit`            IS `in months`
    WHEN `section C -- three months` THEN
        `Period of time` WITH
            `number of units` IS 3
            `unit`            IS `in months`
    WHEN `section D -- six months` THEN
        `Period of time` WITH
            `number of units` IS 6
            `unit`            IS `in months`
    WHEN `section E -- twelve months` THEN
        `Period of time` WITH
            `number of units` IS 12
            `unit`            IS `in months`

-- src:726 | (4) Notwithstanding subsection (1), section 47(2) of the Interpretation Act 1918 and the Second Schedule to that Act shall continue to apply to any Act to which that section applied immediately before the commencement of this Act 4.
@ref s 77(4) (src:726)
GIVEN s IS A `Section of the Second Schedule that fixes a time to complain`
      `the day the offence was committed` IS A DATE
      h IS A `Holidays that make an excluded day`
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the last day to make the complaint and commence the proceedings` s `the day the offence was committed` h MEANS
    CONSIDER `reckon a period` (`the period the Second Schedule section allows` s) `is reckoned from or after the day` `the day the offence was committed` h
    WHEN JUST r THEN JUST (r's `end`'s `last day for doing the thing`)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
